{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: stop the connector's own refused statements from censoring its genuine observations. Separate request rather than a child of 1214a363, because you were right that this is a different defect and should not be smuggled into the authorship one: authorship asks who wrote a statement, this asks whether the fold gave it any force.\n\nI verified it at main 4200aa27. internal/workroom/fold.go:1606 appends to projection.Statements for every record whose body decodes as a State, with no reference to record.decision.Verdict — the verdict is consulted three lines later only to bucket OpaqueKinds. So the projection's statement list carries refused and ineffective statements alongside effective ones, by design, because that list is what lets a reader see what was attempted and why it failed. cmd/gitseq-github/main.go:426 observedStatements then copies that list wholesale into the connector fold.\n\nTHE CONSEQUENCE. A statement the fold refused can still occupy an external id in the correspondence and win first-writer ordering against the connector's real observation. With the authorship guard in place the forger has to be the connector itself, which narrows it sharply — but the connector can produce refused acts like anything else, and a refused act is precisely one the log decided has no force. Letting it suppress a later genuine observation gives force to something the fold denied force to, which is backwards: it is not a forgery problem, it is the projection lending weight to its own rejects.\n\nWHAT I WANT, AND THE ONE THING TO BE CAREFUL OF. Only statements the fold ruled effective should enter the correspondence. The care is in not overcorrecting: the existing first-writer-wins rule between two effective observations must not change, and the connector must still need no private state — the effectiveness of a record is already in the projection, so this is a filter, not a new source of truth. Decide and say in the artifact whether the filter belongs in observedStatements at the adapter, where the projection's decisions are in hand, or inside Fold with effectiveness carried on the Statement shape the way authorship now is. I lean towards the latter for the same reason authorship went there — filtering at one caller leaves the library wrong for the next one — but you are closer to it and I am not mandating the placement.\n\nSequence this behind the authorship work and behind the one-signing-identity child f17681bc; all three touch the same two files and I would rather they land in order than race.","body":{"conditions":"1. A statement the fold did not rule effective cannot enter the correspondence, so it cannot suppress a genuine observation through first-writer ordering. 2. An adversarial test appends an ineffective statement from the connector's own actor carrying source github and an unobserved external_id, and requires the genuine observation still to be emitted; it must fail if the effectiveness filter is removed, verified by making that edit and watching it fail. 3. First-writer-wins between two effective connector observations is unchanged, covered by a test that would fail if the ordering were altered. 4. No private state, disk or I/O added to internal/connector/github; effectiveness comes from the projection the caller already holds. 5. State in the artifact whether the filter sits in the adapter or in Fold, and why. 6. make vet, make test, make race, make build, gofmt and diff-check pass at the exact head; artifact at every maintained path the head changes; independent Architecture/Security/Simplification review before merge.","priority":"P3","sequence_after":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:f17681bcb6e01560498d2124d0664466231e017a","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123","verified_at":"main 4200aa27 on 2026-08-21"}}